    "https://gannon.peopleadmin.com/postings/15196": {
      "title": "Gannon University  | OTD Graduate Assistant (Research) Ruskin",
      "description": "Gannon University  | OTD Graduate Assistant (Research) Ruskin Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Search Position Description Library Log In /Create Account Help OTD Graduate Assistant (Research) Ruskin Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Posting Summary Position Title OTD Graduate Assistant (Research) Ruskin Posting Number AS1096P Subdivision School of Health Professions Division Col.Health Professions & Sci. Department Occupational Therapy Florida Location Ruskin, Fl Position Summary Career Readiness Learning Objectives Career & Self Development: The position provides opportunities for an OTD graduate student to heighten their knowledge and skill set by assuming duties that will help them progress professionally. The recipient of this position will seek and embrace opportunities to assist faculty in research, finding grants and grant writing, and helping to develop and facilitate educational opportunities for both faculty and students. Building and maintaining relationships through daily communications and hands-on support along with seeking and developing learning opportunities are beneficial for professional and personal development. Communication: This position’s requirements include clearly, effectively, and professionally communicating with both students and faculty on the Ruskin campus, both in person and through the various communication media used in the position. Critical Thinking: The position will require the recipient to make decisions and solve problems associated with finding and evaluating relevant research, and using sound, inclusive reasoning and judgement to identify current best evidence associated with healthcare. Gathering and analyzing information from a diverse set of sources and individuals will be necessary. Accurately summarizing and interpreting data with an awareness of personal biases that may impact outcomes will be necessary. Leadership: The position will require an individual who is able to seek out and leverage diverse resources and feedback from others to inform direction for research ideas and to identify best evidence sources that can be used to inform self and others. Professionalism: The position will maintain a professional demeanor when interacting with faculty, other students and potentially small groups from the community. It is essential to demonstrate dependability and the ability to prioritize and complete tasks, as well as the ability to attend to details and to show a high level of dedication toward doing a good job. Equity and Inclusion: The position will require keeping an open mind to diverse ideas and new ways of thinking; to identify resources and eliminate barriers resulting from individual and systemic racism, inequities and biases. Technology: This position requires the use of technology, especially Excel, to improve efficiency and productivity and to complete specific tasks, such as manipulating information, constructing ideas and analyzing research results. We are looking for a detail-oriented student who has an interest in the research process. This position has been generously provided by the Graduate Council to assist Dr. Blair Carsone with research activities, including literature reviews, data collection, data management/analysis, Institutional review Board support, and manuscript and presentation assistance. The research assistant may also be involved in other faculty research or special projects within the OTD department as needed and as time allows. This position requires strong organizational and communication skills, as well as a commitment to professional and ethical behavior. Required Qualifications The preferred candidate will meet the following minimum qualifications: - Must be currently enrolled second-year student for 26-27 academic year in the OTD program in Ruskin, FL. - Cumulative GPA through Fall 2025 of 3.4 or higher; a grade at the time of interview in DOCCT 835 of 85% or higher - Excellent communication skills with peers and faculty - Organized - Attention to detail Preferred Qualifications Proficient in Microsoft Office including Excel Highly organized Experience in research- skill with literature searches and data management Physical Demands Should be able to bend, lift, push and pull to set up lab equipment May be asked to lift up to 50lbs. May be asked to walk campus multiple times a day Exempt Status Non-Exempt Position Classification Support Office Position Type GA (Research, Teaching, Athletic Training) Employment Status Part-Time Position Length 9M Hours per Week 10 Work Schedule Office Hours - Depending On Candidate's Schedule Shift 1st Documents Needed To Apply Required Documents Resume Optional Documents Cover Letter Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Erie, Pennsylvania Campus 109 University Square Erie, Pennsylvania 16541 1-800-GANNON-U or 814-871-7000 Ruskin, Florida Campus 105 Commercial Center Dr. Ruskin, Florida 33573 1-800-GANNON-U or 814-871-7000 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://gannon.peopleadmin.com/postings/14879": {
      "title": "Gannon University  | Makerspace Student Technician I",
      "description": "Gannon University  | Makerspace Student Technician I Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Search Position Description Library Log In /Create Account Help Makerspace Student Technician I Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. For information on this posting please email Nick devine003@gannon.edu Position Details Position Summary Position Title Makerspace Student Technician I Posting Number Division Dean's Office, CEB Department Col. of Engineering & Business Location Erie, Pa Position Summary Career Readiness Learning Objectives: An environment such as the Makerspace also fosters great growth in relation to valuable NACE competencies. Critical Thinking: In the Makerspace, critical thinking and problem-solving skills are crucial. While Lab Tech I is the first position and they are still learning it is imperative to know when they need to ask for help or when they can figure it out themselves. MAJOR ASPECT Communication: Students must articulate their ideas, ask questions, and explain their projects to peers and professors. Communicating with your supervisor is necessary when you have a question. Technology: Makerspaces are technology-driven environments. Makerspace Tech 1s are fresh to the space and will need to learn and effectively comprehend the technologies used on the floor. MAJOR ASPECT Teamwork: Whether it is your internal team or a client, you will be working with others daily. Working with them effectively and efficiently is necessary. Professionalism/Work Ethic: Working in this space requires safety and safety requires you to be professional and diligent at your work. There is no place for misuse or abuse of equipment or your time. Equity and Inclusion: Students can interact with peers to foster intercultural fluency. We also work to bring STEM education to underrepresented populations through outreach. Makerspace Lab Technicians are workers in the I- HACK 6th Floor and 3rd floor labs. They help in the Makerspace by learning how the technology on the floor works and maintaining such equipment. They will learn from the Lab Tech 2’s and use teamwork to properly manage any project or challenge that comes across the floor. They will assist the team in the upkeep of the lab and help Gannon community members in the space. Their main responsibilities will be: Learning the flow of the Makerspace Learning the safe operation of basic equipment and hand tools Maintaining the cleanliness and proper presentation of the floor These responsibilities will be carried out by: Assisting other lab technicians in their daily responsibilities Shadowing other trained individuals in open lab hours Keeping an eye out for tasks that may need to be done in the space Required Qualifications A basic knowledge of subtractive or additive manufacturing, CAD Design, and basic programming skills. Willingness to learn new skills and equipment. Preferred Qualifications Technical trade skills, or manufacturing experience. Physical Demands This position requires the individual to wear and work in personal protective equipment. This position requires that the individual is able to lift a minimum of 50lbs. Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies 2 Job Posting Open Date 08/11/2026 Job Posting Close Date 04/01/2027 Special Instructions to Applicants For information on this posting please email Nick devine003@gannon.edu Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Optional Documents Cover Letter Media File Portfolio Erie, Pennsylvania Campus 109 University Square Erie, Pennsylvania 16541 1-800-GANNON-U or 814-871-7000 Ruskin, Florida Campus 105 Commercial Center Dr. Ruskin, Florida 33573 1-800-GANNON-U or 814-871-7000 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://gannon.peopleadmin.com/postings/15159": {
      "title": "Gannon University  | Senior Maintenance / Building Systems Technician, Ruskin",
      "description": "Gannon University  | Senior Maintenance / Building Systems Technician, Ruskin Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Search Position Description Library Log In /Create Account Help Senior Maintenance / Building Systems Technician, Ruskin Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Posting Summary Position Title Senior Maintenance / Building Systems Technician, Ruskin Posting Number AS1168P Subdivision Campus Services Division Finance and Campus Operations Department Physical Plant, Maintenance Location Ruskin, Fl Position Summary The Senior Maintenance / Building Systems Technician serves as the primary on-site maintenance professional for the Gannon University Ruskin campus and is responsible for routine preventive and corrective maintenance, building inspections, first-line troubleshooting of facility systems, and general repair of University buildings and equipment. The position performs necessary work to maintain a safe, functional, dependable, and welcoming environment for students, faculty, staff, and visitors. This position requires broad commercial building-maintenance knowledge with strength in HVAC and mechanical systems and the ability to work independently. The technician evaluates facility conditions to determine whether work can be both safely and appropriately completed in-house, performs assigned maintenance within training and qualification, and provides informed technical support when qualified specialty contractors required. Major HVAC , electrical, plumbing, fire/life-safety, roofing, access-control, and other specialized work remains outsourced. The Senior Maintenance / Building Systems Technician collaborates closely with the Director of Operations to identify needs, support contractor access, and technical coordination, verify completion of work, and maintain accurate work-order and preventive-maintenance records. Building Maintenance · Perform routine corrective and preventive maintenance on campus buildings, equipment, and furnishings within the scope of training and qualification. · Repair and adjust doors, locks, door hardware, classroom and office furnishings, window blinds, shelving, signage, and similar building components. · Replace ceiling tiles and complete minor drywall, painting, finish, and carpentry repairs. · Install approved fixtures, signage, shelving, artwork, and classroom equipment as authorized by the Director of Operations. · Conduct scheduled building rounds and condition inspections, including occupied areas, unoccupied areas, shell space, mechanical rooms, electrical rooms, roofs from safe accessible locations, and exterior building conditions promptly report significant deficiencies. HVAC and Mechanical Systems · Monitor HVAC and mechanical systems and perform first-line troubleshooting of comfort complaints, alarms, and observed equipment issues. · Review building automation system ( BAS ) information, occupied/unoccupied schedules, alarms, and trends within assigned access and training; communicate significant issues to the Director of Operations and specialty contractors. · Perform assigned routine HVAC preventive maintenance such as filter replacement, visual inspection of equipment, belt inspection/replacement where appropriate, cleaning accessible components, and other manufacturer- or University-approved tasks within training and qualification. · Identify conditions requiring qualified HVAC or controls contractors and provide the Director of Operations with clear technical information to support service calls. · Provide access and on-site technical coordination for HVAC /mechanical contractors and verify that reported work is complete before closing associated work orders. Electrical · Perform minor electrical maintenance within training, qualification, University policy, and applicable safety requirements, including lamp replacement and other approved low-risk tasks. · Perform first-line observation and troubleshooting of lighting and electrical issues and identify conditions requiring a qualified electrician. · Reset breakers or equipment only when authorized, appropriate, and safe to do so; do not perform licensed or high-risk electrical work. Plumbing · Respond to clogged sinks, drains, and toilets and perform minor plumbing repairs within training and qualification. · Identify and contain minor leaks, operate known and clearly identified water shutoffs when necessary, and promptly report conditions requiring a qualified plumber. · Support routine maintenance of plumbing fixtures, plaster traps, water softener components, and similar equipment as assigned. · Provide access and technical coordination for plumbing contractors and verify completion of reported work. Preventive Maintenance and Work Management · Complete assigned preventive maintenance and recurring inspection tasks in accordance with the campus preventive-maintenance schedule. · Maintain accurate and timely work-order records, including problem description, work performed, parts used, contractor involvement, and completion status. · Identify recurring failures, deferred maintenance concerns, equipment condition issues, and potential capital needs and communicate recommendations to the Director of Operations. · Maintain maintenance areas, tools, parts, equipment, and supplies in an organized and safe condition and identify routine inventory needs. Exterior Conditions and Contracted Services Support · Monitor patios, sidewalks, entrances, exterior gathering areas, grounds interfaces, pest/wildlife concerns, and general exterior conditions during routine rounds and report deficiencies requiring custodial, landscaping, pest-control, roofing, or other contracted service. · Address immediate minor hazards or housekeeping conditions when necessary to protect safety or property; routine custodial, landscaping, exterior window washing, and grounds maintenance remain the responsibility of contracted service providers. · Provide contractors with building access, relevant equipment information, and on-site technical coordination as directed by the Director of Operations. Emergency and Life Safety Support · Perform routine visual checks or assigned functional observations of emergency lighting, exit signs, generator status, and other facility conditions as directed; formal inspection, testing, certification, and specialized repair remain with qualified contractors where required. · Provide access and operational support to fire/life-safety contractors and promptly report observed deficiencies to the Director of Operations. · Respond to urgent facility needs during normal work hours and take reasonable make-safe or isolation actions within training and authorization while qualified contractors are dispatched when needed. Event and Campus Support · Provide limited facilities support for major institutional events or activities that require technical assistance, heavy furniture movement, or facilities expertise. · Routine meeting and event room setup, furniture configuration, and teardown are the responsibility of the sponsoring department, students, faculty, or staff unless otherwise approved by the Director of Operations. Administrative Responsibilities · Maintain accurate maintenance, preventive-maintenance, inspection, and contractor-support records using the University’s computerized maintenance management or work-order system. · Assist in maintaining facility key inventories, building-access information, equipment documentation, shutoff information, and other physical plant records as assigned. · Communicate significant building conditions, contractor needs, safety concerns, and capital improvement recommendations to the Director of Operations. · Prioritize daily work based on safety, operational impact, preventive-maintenance requirements, and direction from the Director of Operations. Customer Service · Respond professionally and courteously to faculty, staff, students, contractors, and visitors. · Maintain positive and collaborative working relationships with campus departments and contracted service providers. · Communicate status and expected next steps on maintenance issues in a timely manner. After-Hours Emergency Response · The normal work schedule is Monday through Friday. The position may occasionally require outside normal business hours for significant facilities emergencies in accordance with University procedures and applicable call-back requirements. · Routine maintenance requests occurring outside normal work hours are addressed during the next appropriate work period. · The Director of Operations provides management-level backup and coordinates qualified emergency contractors when the technician is unavailable. The position will not provide routine work to support after-hours campus events. Required Qualifications · High school diploma or GED required; technical or trade education strongly preferred. · Five years of progressively responsible experience in commercial, institutional, educational, healthcare, or similar facilities maintenance, or an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience. · Demonstrated experience troubleshooting commercial HVAC /mechanical systems and performing routine preventive maintenance on building equipment. · Working knowledge of general plumbing, electrical safety and basic troubleshooting, carpentry, painting, doors/hardware, and general commercial building maintenance. · Ability to independently evaluate a facility problem, determine whether work can be safely completed in-house, and identify when qualified specialty contractors are required. · Ability to safely operate hand tools, power tools, ladders, lifts, and maintenance equipment appropriate to assigned work. · Basic computer skills and ability to use electronic work-order/ CMMS systems. · Valid Florida driver’s license with an acceptable driving record. · Ability to pass required background screening. · Candidates must be able to support and promote the University’s Mission and the Catholic Intellectual Tradition. Preferred Qualifications · EPA Section 608 certification. · Experience with building automation systems ( BAS ), commercial controls, or energy-management systems. · Trade certification or formal technical training in HVAC , mechanical systems, electrical, plumbing, or building maintenance. · Experience using computerized maintenance management systems ( CMMS ). · Experience in higher education, healthcare, laboratory, or institutional facilities. · Strong troubleshooting, mechanical aptitude, and problem-solving skills. · Ability to prioritize multiple assignments and respond to changing operational needs. · Ability to work independently with minimal supervision and communicate when escalation is required. · Excellent interpersonal and customer-service skills. · Knowledge of OSHA safety standards, lockout/tagout principles, and safe work practices appropriate to assigned duties. · Ability to communicate effectively with specialty contractors and verify that contracted work addresses the reported condition. · Ability to respond to emergency situations and safely access affected areas consistent with assigned responsibilities. Physical Demands · Frequently stand, walk, bend, kneel, climb ladders, and work in mechanical and building-support spaces. · Lift, carry, push, or pull up to 50 pounds regularly and occasionally up to 75 pounds with assistance. · Work both indoors and outdoors in varying weather conditions. Exempt Status Non-Exempt Position Classification Professional Technician Position Type Staff Employment Status Full-Time Position Length 12M Hours per Week 40 Work Schedule normal operating hours, with the ocassional need for evening and weekend support Shift 1st Documents Needed To Apply Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Erie, Pennsylvania Campus 109 University Square Erie, Pennsylvania 16541 1-800-GANNON-U or 814-871-7000 Ruskin, Florida Campus 105 Commercial Center Dr. Ruskin, Florida 33573 1-800-GANNON-U or 814-871-7000 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://gannon.peopleadmin.com/postings/15098": {
      "title": "Gannon University",
      "description": "Gannon University  | STEM Center - Tutor - Major: Cyber Eng, Cyber Sec Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Search Position Description Library Log In /Create Account Help STEM Center - Tutor - Major: Cyber Eng, Cyber Sec Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. In order to be considered, you must fill out supplementary information and upload resume at this Microsoft Forms questionnaire: https://forms.cloud.microsoft/r/3LmrskNZYV This position carries a Federal Work Study eligible preference, but it is possible that other students can be hired. Previous STEM Center experience and previous STEM Center application is preferred. Position Details Position Summary Position Title STEM Center - Tutor - Major: Cyber Eng, Cyber Sec Posting Number Division Library Department Provost & Student Experience (AA) Location Erie, Pa Position Summary Believe in your own knowledge and perseverance, and the amazing potential of Gannon students Lead Gannon students to conquer their academic hurdles, both small and large Unleash your love for your major and your favorite courses Enjoy making a deep impact on a very flexible schedule Key responsibilities and highlights: Tutor peers in STEM subject material they are qualified to cover May seek or perform other necessary STEM Center work, with approval Some initial and ongoing (paid) training is likely to be required Position structure: Will have one regular paid guaranteed shift per week (likely 3 hours, and in the late afternoon and early evening such as 4-7PM on a Sunday-Thursday); work during this shift will be tutoring, preparation/training, other tasks to support the STEM Center and our stakeholders, or other work to benefit the University Needs to establish additional availability within library hours of operation for “on-call” tutoring (paid for actual tutoring appointments only; up to ~3 additional hours if holding appointments, more may be possible but cannot be guaranteed) There will basically be one such tutor per major (one per set of similar majors) for each area traditionally served by the STEM Center Career Readiness Learning Objectives: Critical Thinking/Problem Solving: A STEM tutor can develop critical thinking and problem-solving skills by breaking down complex concepts for students, identifying gaps in their understanding, and helping them develop strategies to solve problems. Oral/Written Communication: STEM tutors need to clearly explain complex ideas and concepts to students within tutoring sessions and in interactions with coworkers and STEM Center management. Tutors may be required to speak to classes, reach out to students, and represent the STEM Center at functions or events. Additionally, tutors may be required to send emails, make phone calls, or initiate other communication on behalf of the STEM Center. Teamwork/Collaboration: STEM tutors can facilitate or assist with group study sessions and participate in other projects or initiatives to support the STEM Center. Digital Technology: Tutors may create online resources, utilizing software or apps to visualize concepts, and must make extensive use of scheduling systems, MS Teams, and other software to perform essential functions of the job. Leadership: In working with a variety of students, tutors have regular opportunity to thrive in changing environments, to identify and address complex problems, and to navigate ambiguity. Professionalism/Work Ethic: By being punctual, prepared, and professional in their interactions with students, tutors can showcase a strong work ethic. They must also adhere to ethical guidelines and respect the confidentiality of their students. Career Management: The STEM Center is a cocurricular opportunity to develop and showcase the ability to build success in others and specifically to teach or train others. Tutoring course material often builds additional competencies in those areas. Global/Intercultural Fluency: To develop global and intercultural fluency, tutors often have opportunities to work with a diverse slate of Gannon students, learning, understanding, and adapting to academic and nonacademic facets of culture and communication. Required Qualifications Major and significant course load matching position title High GPA and positive reputation with faculty Interpersonal excellence Preferred Qualifications Physical Demands N/A Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies 1 Job Posting Open Date 08/14/2026 Job Posting Close Date 04/30/2027 Special Instructions to Applicants In order to be considered, you must fill out supplementary information and upload resume at this Microsoft Forms questionnaire: https://forms.cloud.microsoft/r/3LmrskNZYV This position carries a Federal Work Study eligible preference, but it is possible that other students can be hired. Previous STEM Center experience and previous STEM Center application is preferred. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Have you or will you soon complete the REQUIRED questionnaire at: https://forms.cloud.microsoft/r/3LmrskNZYV ? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Optional Documents Erie, Pennsylvania Campus 109 University Square Erie, Pennsylvania 16541 1-800-GANNON-U or 814-871-7000 Ruskin, Florida Campus 105 Commercial Center Dr. Ruskin, Florida 33573 1-800-GANNON-U or 814-871-7000 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://gannon.peopleadmin.com/postings/15218": {
      "title": "Gannon University",
      "description": "Gannon University  | SSS/TRIO - Mentor Aide - Graduate Level Student Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Search Position Description Library Log In /Create Account Help SSS/TRIO - Mentor Aide - Graduate Level Student Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Resume, cover letter, letters of recommendation (2) Position Details Position Summary Position Title SSS/TRIO - Mentor Aide - Graduate Level Student Posting Number Division Retention & Student Services Department Provost & Student Experience (AA) Location Erie, Pa Position Summary Career Readiness Learning Objectives: Critical Thinking - Mentors assist mentees in making decisions that promote academic, personal, and professional growth. Communication- Effectively conveying information in various formats. Student Support Services ( TRIO ) seeks an empathic socially-skilled graduate student, particularly one who can relate to the background(s) of our students, to fill this position, with a strong preference for students with training and/or experience working with college students. This position will: Assist with contacting incoming first-year students to explain the program and serve as a resource prior to the start of courses Provide data entry support, assemble files, and assist with filing Meet individually with current SSS students as needed Plan and collaborate to facilitate workshops Share knowledge and information related to applying to graduate school Research scholarship opportunities for SSS students Collaborate with staff to design and host program events Introduce SSS students to campus and community resources Be a student leader for SSS Peer Mentors Required Qualifications Ability to work as a team Ability to operate standard office equipment Adept at Microsoft Suite, Canva, and Adobe Pro Ability and comfort in learning to utilize new software other duties, as assigned Preferred Qualifications Preference will be given to those with experience participating in or working with TRIO programs (e.g., Upward Bound, Talent Search, Student Support Services and/or McNair Scholars Program) Physical Demands Must be able to meet the physical demands associated with a busy office and University environment Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies 1 Job Posting Open Date 08/03/2026 Job Posting Close Date 09/11/2026 Special Instructions to Applicants Resume, cover letter, letters of recommendation (2) Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * What do you know about TRIO Student Support Services? (Open Ended Question) * Can you commit to a full academic year as TRIO SSS Graduate Mentor Aide? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Letter of Recommendation Optional Documents Erie, Pennsylvania Campus 109 University Square Erie, Pennsylvania 16541 1-800-GANNON-U or 814-871-7000 Ruskin, Florida Campus 105 Commercial Center Dr. Ruskin, Florida 33573 1-800-GANNON-U or 814-871-7000 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
